---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PregnancyWeekCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/PregnancyWeekCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pregnancy-week-calculator">
  <PregnancyWeekCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the pregnancy week calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The pregnancy week calculator counts the days from the start of the pregnancy to today and divides them
      by 7. Pregnancy weeks are counted from the first day of the last period, about two weeks before
      conception. A full pregnancy is 280 days, or 40 weeks.
    </p>
    <p>
      The start is <code>start = LMP + (cycle − 28) days</code>, where <code>LMP</code> is the first day of the
      last period and <code>cycle</code> is your usual cycle length in days. A longer cycle means later
      ovulation, so the start moves later. Then <code>days = today − start</code>, the weeks pregnant are
      <code>days ÷ 7</code> with the remainder as days, and the due date is <code>start + 280 days</code>. If
      you enter a due date, the start is <code>due date − 280 days</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first trimester is weeks 0 to 13, the second is weeks 14 to 27 and the third is week 28 to birth.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      The last period started on 1 June 2026 and the cycle is 28 days. On 2 November 2026 that is 154 days, or
      22 weeks 0 days: the second trimester. The due date is 8 March 2027, 126 days later. With a 32-day cycle
      the start moves to 5 June, the count is 21 weeks 3 days and the due date is 12 March 2027.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The cycle correction assumes ovulation 14 days before the next period, so irregular cycles give less exact
      dates. A first-trimester ultrasound dates a pregnancy more exactly than the period date. If your clinic
      gave you a due date, enter that date. The milestone weeks on the timeline are typical; your clinic sets
      your own schedule.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
